# SETJMP(3)

## NAME

setjmp, longjmp - non-local goto

## SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <setjmp.h>

int setjmp(jmp_buf env);

void longjmp(jmp_buf env, int val);
```

## DESCRIPTION

These routines are useful for dealing with errors and interrupts encountered in a low-level
subroutine of a program.

*Setjmp* saves its stack environment in *env* for later use by *longjmp.* It returns value 0.

*Longjmp* restores the environment saved by the last call of *setjmp*. It then returns in such
a way that execution continues as if the call of *setjmp* had just returned the value *val* to
the function that invoked *setjmp,* which must not itself have returned in the interim. All
accessible data have values as of the time *longjmp* was called.

**Note:** *longjmp* is deliberately *not* declared **_Noreturn**, which is <setjmp.h>'s one
departure from C11 sec 7.13.2.1. Declaring it so would turn every call site into a tail jump
for no gain here, and no caller expects it back in any case.

## SEE ALSO

signal(2)

## FILES

| share/besm6/lib/libc.a
| lib/libc/gen/setjmp.s, include/setjmp.h

## BESM-6 NOTES

### Written from the calling convention, not ported

v7's is x86 assembly in the port these sources came from, and the c-compiler's library has none
at all. It is the same problem the kernel's context switch solves one level down, minus
everything that made that hard: no u-area to copy, no address space to swap, no interrupts to
mask.

### The buffer

**jmp_buf** is **int[8]**, as on every v7 machine, but what goes into it is this machine's, and
only setjmp.s may touch it:

> ```
> 0     r13         the return address into setjmp's caller
> 1     r15         the stack pointer at the call
> 2,3,4 r5,r6,r7    the callee-saved registers compiled code uses
> 5     R           the AU mode register
> 6,7   - unused -
> ```

It is **int** and not **char**, so a **jmp_buf** is a plain word address rather than a fat
pointer and goes straight into an index register.

### Only r5..r7 of the callee-saved set

The convention says r1..r7 must be preserved, but the compiler-support prologue saves exactly
r5, r6 and r7, and compiled code allocates nothing below r5 - what a disassembly shows is r6
and r7 as the parameter and auto pointers, r8..r12 as expression scratch, and r13/r14/r15. A
function that clobbered r1..r4 across a call would already break an ordinary return, so there
is nothing here for *setjmp* to save that the prologue does not.

### Neither routine has a prologue

*Setjmp* has one parameter, so it arrives in the accumulator, nothing is pushed, and r15 at
entry is already the caller's: a callee whose arguments were all in registers pops nothing.
*Longjmp* never returns, so its one pushed argument is never popped - r15 comes from the
buffer.

*Longjmp* restores the index registers with **wtc**/**vtm**, which is why *val* never has to
leave the accumulator.

### Why R is saved at all

The ABI fixes R = 7 at every call boundary, so the R a *longjmp* is entered with is by
construction the R its *setjmp* is entitled to return with - the slot ought to be unnecessary.
It is kept because a program may *longjmp out of a signal handler,* and a handler is not a call
boundary. It costs one word. lib/test/signals ends on exactly that case: sleep(3), whose
handler leaves by *longjmp* and never returns through the frame at all.
